concepts.body:
Much of programming is about plain data: a point with x and y, a user with a name and an email. Writing `case` in front of a class tells the compiler to generate all the routine code such a data type needs. That repetitive code is called **boilerplate**, and with a **case class** you never write it:

- constructor parameters become public `val`s;
- `==` compares the field values, so two `Point(1, 2)` are equal; this is called structural equality, and `hashCode`, a number used to find values quickly in sets and maps, agrees with it;
- a readable `toString`: `Point(1,2)`;
- a `copy` method: `p.copy(y = 5)` gives a new point with only `y` changed;
- a companion `apply` and `unapply`, so you can create instances without `new` and take them apart with pattern matching (below).

Case classes are the default way to represent data in Scala. They are immutable, so "updating" one means making a `copy`, which is cheap because the unchanged fields are shared, not duplicated.

Because equality compares contents, case classes work well as keys in a `Map` (a lookup table from keys to values) or as items in a `Set` (a collection with no duplicates).

A **case object** is the single-instance version, useful as one fixed option among several. A case class with no fields still needs empty parentheses: `case class Tick()`.

Pattern matching, covered in the next lesson, can take a case class apart: `p match { case Point(0, y) => ... }` checks that x is 0 and gives the name `y` to the y value.

Case classes also let libraries list their field names (`productElementNames`), which tools that convert data to and from text build on. Don't build one case class on top of another; to model several alternatives of one thing, such as circles and squares as kinds of shape, use a `sealed trait` or an `enum`, covered later.

